## Lessons
Each lesson has the observation, what it changes in practice, and the status now.

### L1. Installed bytes, stored bytes, tracked custody
Observation: R02 and R03. A worktree hash can pass while the index or the commit
differs. Source: Pack A return `#04`, Pack A Prime report, Pack B `#B-A02` and
`#B-A03`, finding A-F1.
Practice: check attributes, worktree, index and commit with `gitbytes.py` and an
explicit manifest. Do not treat a clean diff as the check.
Status: closed for this workspace's custody policy. The archive-branch helper is
still the earlier one. This batch checks its own files and does not change that helper.

### L2. Bounded tools, preflight, journals, retained failures
Observation: R04. Pack A also records a builder-caused interruption-test fault,
then a full re-run, and a skipped symlink test (WinError 1314) that is not a pass.
Journals in the preservation lab are `complete`. Source: Pack A return `#02` and
`#03`, Pack B return `#02`, handoff `REPORT.md`, Prime B report.
Practice: keep the failed attempt. A skip is not a pass. A tool's expected refusal
inside a negative test is evidence.
Status: retained. Those suites were not re-run here. Their evidence stays where it is.

### L3. Authoritative assignment and derived `00.live`
Observation: R05. The view names the record hash and the register hash. `check`
refuses a view that is not the function of those inputs. It does not resolve the
book, the space, or the work item. Source: `render_live.py`, Prime PR-B01,
handoff report item 4, paper section 6 as the intended design only.
Practice: regenerate or check the view after a record edit. Do not read the view
as permission. Do not describe a matching view as proof the named job exists.
Status: open as PR-B01. No resolver was added.

### L4. Expected refusal and operational failure
Observation: the gate's refusals are the passing cases (R03). Law draft `0065`
says stop when a tool refuses. Prime PR-B02 says an expected negative-test refusal
is evidence, and an unexpected operational refusal, an unsafe condition, a missing
dependency, or a scope conflict still stops the action.
Practice: inside this batch, an expected difference is recorded and the batch
continues. An unexpected stop still stops that action.
Status: proposal only. `0065` was not edited. Applied as the working rule of this
released batch, not as seated law.

### L5. Role, model, and standing assignment
Observation: R06. The Director's release says to work as Builder and to record
the claim. It does not say the standing seat is filled for later jobs. The paper,
sections 4 and 5, describes roles as contracts and routine delegation. That is
research context, not a grant.
Practice: record the model, the session and the release. Do not invent power past
the batch. Do not send a routine edit, already inside the batch, back to the
Director as if it were a new contract.
Status: `0408.seats/01.md` records Grok 4.7 scoped to this batch. Law `0063` and
`0065`, and `AGENTS.004.md`, were not edited.

### L6. Imported reference, local authority, versions and hashes
Observation: README and the Pack B return separate imported reference from local
authority. The selection register holds 73 reference faces and 8 inactive
candidates. Prime PR-B04: a stamp-only move can keep code bytes; a new resolver
or a new selection rule cannot. The next pack has to authorize the body edits,
write a new candidate version, and keep both hashes.
Practice: do not execute a candidate, do not seat a face, and do not copy a paper
hypothesis into a status line.
Status: open. F04, F05 and PR-B04. No candidate file was edited.

### L7. Development `05.NN` and a final `05`
Observation: seed N08, this page's `01.md`, and paper section 7. A final `05`
is compiled after the method has been exercised and reviewed. Director replies
on `0409.decisions/01.md` stay quoted.
Practice: this file's `05.01.md` remains the development batch. Do not replace it
with a cleaned instruction, and do not drop the release wording.
Status: no final `05` in this batch. No why book. The Director's question in the
notes stays a question.

### L8. Prime's judgment inside a delegation
Observation: the notes' report contract, and Prime's Pack B report. Prime accepted
establishment and left activation, seating, lock, `main`, and the next pack
unapproved. PR-B03 disposes B-F1, B-F2 and B-F3 without a new decision queue.
Changes of purpose, authority, shared contract, or scope still escalate.
Practice: this return asks Codex Prime to review this revision. It does not ask
the Director to arbitrate B-F1, and it does not release finish line 2.
Status: recorded on `0611.findings/01.md`. The law text was not changed.

### L9. Open runtime, Clerk and lint
Observation: findings F01, F04 and F05. README: no seated loader, no Clerk route,
no lock. The handoff lab is a scripted eight-process loop with a kept failure,
not live-model routing and not an approved store. Paper section 9, at its
snapshot, does not know Prime's later acceptance. Current status is the Prime
report plus the inactive candidates, not the paper's snapshot and not a claim
that the desk now runs.
Practice: writing this page does not mark those issues fixed.
Status: open. Next work is the fixture slice, proposed and not released.

